A Common Table Expression, or CTE, is essentially a temporary, named result set that you define at the very beginning of a SQL query using the WITH clause.
It acts exactly like a temporary view that exists only for the duration of that specific query execution.

Technical Breakdown :-
Readability & Flow: Instead of burying a complex subquery deep inside a FROM or JOIN clause where you have to read the SQL inside-out,
a CTE lets you read the logic top-to-bottom. You define WITH Step_1 AS (...), then Step_2 AS (...), and finally SELECT * FROM Step_2.

Reusability: If you need to join the same aggregated sub-dataset twice in the same main query,
a subquery forces you to write and compute that logic twice. A CTE lets you define it once at the top, and refer to it multiple times below.

Recursion: CTEs have a unique superpower called Recursive CTEs. By referencing itself,
a recursive CTE can traverse hierarchical data—like finding all the direct and indirect reports of a CEO in an employee table—which is practically impossible with standard subqueries.

Business Context :-

In data engineering, our SQL scripts often grow to hundreds of lines long, powering critical dbt models or BI dashboards. If an analyst inherits a massive query written entirely with nested subqueries, debugging it is a nightmare.
CTEs enforce modular, declarative programming within SQL, radically reducing maintenance time and cognitive load for the team.

Extra Edge:-
I have to watch out for performance traps. While CTEs make code cleaner, they are not indexed.
In engines like PostgreSQL (prior to version 12), CTEs acted as optimization fences, meaning the query planner couldn't push filters down into them, causing massive full-table scans in memory.
Today, modern engines like Snowflake or BigQuery are smart enough to inline and optimize CTEs just like subqueries, but if I'm reusing a massively heavy CTE 5 times in one query,
I might refactor it into an actual temporary table to avoid recomputing it repeatedly.
